\begin{center}
\large
Abstract
\end{center}

In this paper we explore connections between Mansfield's work on differential Gröbner bases and
Boulier's work on differential radical ideals. In particular, we obtain a generalization of the result
in differential algebra known as Rosenfeld's Lemma. This generalization replaces Rosenfeld's
`auto-reduced' hypothesis (very effectively used by Boulier in his computation of radical
differential ideals) with Mansfield's weaker `almost complete' hypothesis, and thus permits some
of Boulier's techniques to be adapted to the more general case. 

